---
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import DemoLayout from '../../layouts/DemoLayout.astro';

const numbers: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: 120 }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index + 1);

const listExample: string = `<input type="checkbox" id="primes-only">

<ol class="numbers">
    <li></li>
    <li></li>
    <li></li>
    <!-- … and so on, up to 120 -->
</ol>`;

const numberExample: string = `.numbers { counter-reset: number; }
.numbers li { counter-increment: number; }
.numbers li::before { content: counter(number); }

.numbers li:first-child { visibility: hidden; }`;

const crossingExample: string = `.numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)) {
    background: var(--brand-amber);
}

.numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)) {
    background: var(--brand-rose);
}

.numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)) {
    background: var(--brand-blue);
}

.numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
    background: var(--brand-green);
}`;

const primesExample: string = `.sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)),
.sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)),
.sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)),
.sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
    visibility: hidden;
}`;
---
<DemoLayout path="/Demos/Sieve">
    <Fragment slot="what">
        <p>
            Around 2,200 years ago, the Greek mathematician Eratosthenes described a way to find prime numbers without
            dividing anything. Write out the numbers; take the first prime, 2, and cross out all its multiples; move to the
            next number not crossed out, which is the next prime, and cross out its multiples; and carry on. Whatever is
            left is prime.
        </p>
        <p>
            Below, CSS has done the crossing out: each colour marks the multiples of one prime, and the numbers left in plain
            type are the primes from 2 to 113. Tick the box to hide everything that was crossed out.
        </p>
    </Fragment>

    <div slot="demo" class="sieve">
        <div class="sieve-controls">
            <label class="primes-toggle">
                <input type="checkbox" id="primes-only" />
                Show the primes only
            </label>
            <ul class="legend" role="list">
                <li class="legend-2">Multiple of 2</li>
                <li class="legend-3">Multiple of 3</li>
                <li class="legend-5">Multiple of 5</li>
                <li class="legend-7">Multiple of 7</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <ol class="numbers" role="list" aria-label="The numbers 1 to 120">
            {numbers.map(() => <li></li>)}
        </ol>
    </div>

    <Fragment slot="how">
        <p>
            The HTML is a checkbox and an ordered list of 120 empty list items. As in <a href="/Demos/FizzBuzz">FizzBuzz</a>,
            CSS numbers the items with a counter and finds multiples with <code>:nth-child()</code>. The sieve adds two things:
            an exception for the primes themselves, and a checkbox that changes what's shown.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">The HTML</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{listExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Numbering</h3>
        <p>
            The counter goes up by one on every item, and each item prints it. The first item is 1, which is neither prime nor a
            multiple of one, so it's made invisible rather than removed: it still takes up its place, so every other item's
            position in the list stays the same as its number.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Numbering source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{numberExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Crossing out</h3>
        <p>
            Each prime, <em>P</em>, has one rule. <code>:nth-child(Pn)</code> matches every multiple of it, and
            <code>:not(:nth-child(P))</code> leaves out the prime itself. So <code>:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2))</code>
            crosses out 4, 6, 8 and so on, but not 2. Whatever no rule matches is prime.
        </p>
        <p>
            A number with more than one prime factor, such as 30, is matched by several rules. The rules are in the order 2, 3, 5
            and 7, and the last one to match wins, so 30 takes the colour for 5.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Crossing out source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{crossingExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Showing only the primes</h3>
        <p>
            The checkbox is the only thing the sieve remembers. <code>:has()</code> lets the sieve as a whole see whether it's
            ticked, and if it is, everything that was crossed out is hidden. The tests are the same ones again: a number that was
            crossed out by a rule is hidden by the matching rule.
        </p>
        <Disclosure>
            <Fragment slot="summary">Showing only the primes source</Fragment>
            <pre><code>{primesExample}</code></pre>
        </Disclosure>

        <h3>Why four primes are enough</h3>
        <p>
            Every number up to 120 that isn't prime has a prime factor no bigger than its square root, and the square root of 120
            is just under 11. So crossing out the multiples of 2, 3, 5 and 7 catches all of them. A longer list needs more primes:
            to go to 1,000, you'd add rules for 11 up to 31.
        </p>

        <h3>The end result</h3>
        <p>
            Every number that a smaller prime divides is coloured in, and the numbers left in plain type are the primes from 2 to
            113. Ticking the box leaves just those on show.
        </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="why">
        <p>
            This is a real algorithm, more than two thousand years old, carried out from start to finish by rules that only
            describe what each number should look like. But there's a catch. In a program, the sieve finds each next prime
            itself and carries on. Here, someone had to know which primes to write rules for. The CSS carries out the algorithm,
            but it can't extend itself: it computes within limits a person set in advance.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
</DemoLayout>

<style>
    .sieve {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.25rem;
    }

    .sieve-controls {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 1rem;
    }

    .primes-toggle {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.6rem;
        color: var(--heading);
        font-weight: 500;
        cursor: pointer;
    }

    .primes-toggle input {
        width: 1.25rem;
        height: 1.25rem;
        accent-color: var(--brand-blue);
    }

    .legend {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 0.5rem;
        padding: 0;
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }

    .legend li {
        padding: 0.2rem 0.7rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
        corner-shape: round;
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .numbers {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(2.75rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.4rem;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        counter-reset: number;
    }

    .numbers li {
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--heading);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        font-weight: 600;
        counter-increment: number;
    }

    .numbers li::before {
        content: counter(number);
    }

    /* 1 is neither prime nor crossed out; it keeps its place so every other number keeps its position. */
    .numbers li:first-child {
        visibility: hidden;
    }

    .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
        font-weight: 400;
    }

    /* Each colour's text is whichever of black and white reads better on it. */
    .legend .legend-2,
    .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)) {
        background: var(--brand-amber);
        color: #000;
    }

    .legend .legend-3,
    .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)) {
        background: var(--brand-rose);
        color: #000;
    }

    .legend .legend-5,
    .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)) {
        background: var(--brand-blue);
        color: #fff;
    }

    .legend .legend-7,
    .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
        background: var(--brand-green);
        color: #000;
    }

    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
        visibility: hidden;
    }
</style>
